{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["The word of Abraham son of Meir Ibn Ezra the Spaniard: This is the book of the desired man, in which precious things are spoken, and prophecies past and future. And everything is stated briefly in riddles, and in angels on high, secrets, standing on foundations, and its interpreters have not stood in its secret, and each explained as his hand reached, and the feet of all of them slip, at the appointed end of times, and also the beast became intermingled with them, for its name fourth is the third. And now I begin to interpret according to the precision of the language." "<b>In the year</b> — We find written that Jehoiakim reigned eleven years, so what is the reason \"in the third year of his kingdom\"?! And now, these words \"the sons of Josiah\": it is written that he left four sons: the firstborn Johanan, the second Jehoiakim, the third Zedekiah, the fourth Shallum. And we find that at Josiah's death his son reigned, and some of our Sages, of blessed memory, said that this Jehoahaz is Johanan, and behold he reigned only three months, and his brother Jehoiakim reigned in his place, and he was twenty-three years old, and behold he is younger than Jehoiakim, and how does Scripture call him firstborn?! Therefore they said: he was firstborn in kingship, and they needed to say that Shallum is Zedekiah, because the prophecy of Jeremiah concerning Shallum clung to his prophecy concerning Zedekiah. And furthermore: because no one reigned over Israel after Zedekiah, and for Scripture to count him fourth for the name's sake without a body is a strange way. And now consider that Jehoahaz is Shallum, and Johanan did not reign, and do not be troubled because he is called Shallum by two names, for behold Solomon is Koheleth, and the sons of Samuel are Vashni and Abijah, and Scripture says that he is Joel, and Amminadab is Izhar, and Uriel is Zephaniah, and many likewise. And in the Torah Zerah is Zohar. And the proof that Shallum is Jehoahaz is that when the father died, Jehoiakim was twenty-five years old and Zedekiah, who is the third, twenty-four, and Shallum twenty-three, and thus it is written that Jehoahaz is Shallum, and the people of the land took Jehoahaz son of Josiah and anointed him and made him king instead of Josiah his father. And furthermore, secondly: that Shallum is Jehoahaz, for it is written that Pharaoh Necho took him after three months that he reigned and brought him to Egypt and he died there, and it is written in Jeremiah concerning Shallum: he who went out from this place shall not return there anymore, for in the place where they exiled him there he shall die. And do not wonder because of what is written above: \"The word which came to Jeremiah from YHWH when King Zedekiah sent to him\" — for the beginning of the pericope is: \"For thus said YHWH concerning the house of the king of Judah\" — it recounts what HaShem said to him before Zedekiah reigned, and behold it recounts what he prophesied concerning Jehoahaz, who is Shallum, after they led him to Egypt, and it begins from \"Woe to him who builds his house without righteousness\" — concerning Jehoiakim, and there it is written, and after that he prophesied concerning Jehoiachin, for thus it is written there, and all these three reigned before Zedekiah. And in the book of Ezekiel, where he says: \"Take up a lamentation for the princes of Israel\" who died and from whose sons no king remained, and he begins from Jehoahaz, who is Shallum, and says: \"and they brought him with hooks to the land of Egypt,\" and \"the lion\" is Jehoiakim, whom the king of Egypt made king, and \"he put him in a cage with hooks and brought him to the king of Babylon,\" and thus it is written in Chronicles: \"and they bound him in bronze fetters to lead him to Babylon.\" And after that he laments over Jehoiachin: \"Your mother was like a vine; fire consumed it,\" and after that he laments over Zedekiah: \"and fire came out from a rod in its branches and devoured its fruit, and it had no strong rod, a scepter to rule.\" And behold it has been established that Jehoiakim was led to Babylon, and let it not be difficult in your eyes because it says \"to lead him to Babylon\" and does not say \"and they led him,\" for behold it is written concerning Zedekiah in Jeremiah: \"and they bound him in bronze fetters to bring him to Babylon,\" and afterward it is written: \"and they bound him in bronze fetters and brought him to Babylon.\" And behold it is written in Daniel: \"In the third year of the kingdom of Jehoiakim king of Judah, Nebuchadnezzar came to Jerusalem and besieged it, and YHWH gave into his hand Jehoiakim king of Judah and some of the vessels of the house of HaShem, and he brought them to the land of Shinar.\" And behold the meaning is: Jehoiakim and the vessels he brought into his god's house to bow down to him, and after that it says: \"and the vessels he brought into the treasury of his god,\" and it is not possible by way of the language to interpret \"and he brought them\" as the vessels alone, because of what is written: \"and the vessels he brought.\" And it is written in Chronicles: \"and they bound him in bronze fetters to lead him to Babylon, and some of the vessels of the house of HaShem Nebuchadnezzar brought to Babylon and put them in his temple in Babylon,\" and Jeremiah prophesies concerning Jehoiakim: \"the burial of a donkey shall he be buried, dragged and cast beyond the gates of Jerusalem.\" Therefore the one who said that \"in the third year of the kingdom of Jehoiakim\" whom Pharaoh made king is the third year of his rebellion erred, and therefore I needed to explain that in the third year of the kingdom of Jehoiakim whom Pharaoh made king, Nebuchadnezzar came against him and besieged Jerusalem and took it at the beginning of the fourth year of Jehoiakim, and that is the first year of Nebuchadnezzar, for thus it is written, and they led him to Babylon and he stood in bronze for about a year, and Nebuchadnezzar commanded and Jehoiakim returned to his kingship: \"and Jehoiakim became his servant three years, then he turned and rebelled against him,\" and Nebuchadnezzar or his troops came to Jerusalem and killed Jehoiakim and dragged him beyond the gates of Jerusalem, and his corpse was cast out and not buried, for thus Jeremiah prophesied. And do not wonder because of \"and Jehoiakim slept with his fathers,\" for behold it is written: \"you shall sleep with your fathers,\" and they died in Egypt. And behold Jehoiachin his son reigned three months, and Nebuchadnezzar seized them in the eighth year of his reign, and that is the eleventh year of Jehoiakim, and Zedekiah reigned eleven years, and that is the nineteenth year of King Nebuchadnezzar. And behold in three months Jehoiakim was killed and Jehoiachin his son exiled, and do not wonder because you find written: \"in the seventh year of Nebuchadnezzar,\" and likewise concerning Zedekiah it is the eighteenth year, for in the counting of kings there are such and such. Now we know that Jeroboam and Rehoboam reigned in one month, and behold it is written that Rehoboam reigned seventeen years and Abijah his son reigned in his place in the eighteenth year of Jeroboam. And behold I shall explain this to you: one reckoning counts from the exodus from Egypt in Nisan, or from the creation of the world in Tishri, and one day in a year is counted as a year. It counts from the day the king began to reign until the turn of his year, therefore the eighteenth year becomes the nineteenth. And the parable: one born on the sixth day before sunset in this city, which is at the summer solstice in Tammuz, and the day is sixteen full hours long — behold the son is circumcised on the morning of the sixth day, which is the eighth day according to the Torah's reckoning, and it is the seventh day and is not complete, rather it lacks sixteen hours. And do not wonder because you find in Ezekiel: \"he shall bring him into strongholds so that his voice is not heard,\" for thus the servants of Nebuchadnezzar planned. And the reason for the inquiry that Jeremiah asked Jehoiakim, for the prophecy concerning him — \"do you think that your kingship will be established for you because you compete with cedar?\" And behold the difficult question remains: I have already mentioned that Zedekiah was twenty-four years old at his father's death, and so he would be thirty-five at the death of Jehoiakim and the exile of Jehoiachin, and behold it is written: \"Zedekiah was twenty-one years old when he reigned,\" and this reckoning is that he was called king three years before Josiah's death, for it became known among all the nations that Zedekiah was made king, and the proof is what is written in Jeremiah: \"at the beginning of the kingdom of Jehoiakim son of Josiah king of Judah, the word of HaShem came to Jeremiah, that he should make bonds and send them to the kings of Edom, Moab, Ammon, and Tyre, by the hand of the messengers coming to Jerusalem to Zedekiah king of Judah,\" for these kings heard that Josiah was killed and thought that Zedekiah, whom they made king when he was twenty-one years old, would reign in his place, and there is no argument from the kingship of Jehoahaz, for the people of the land made him king and they did not hear his words, for he reigned only three months. And there remains in the pericope one thing to explain, namely: \"and to Zedekiah king of Judah I spoke,\" and behold Jeremiah recounts this matter of the bonds to Zedekiah at the time he reigned after the exile of Jehoiachin, and said to him: \"Bring your necks under the yoke of the king of Babylon,\" and there it is written: \"and it came to pass in that year, at the beginning of the kingdom of Zedekiah king of Judah.\" And the meaning of \"in the fourth year\" is that Jehoiakim rebelled against the king of Babylon. And there is one emendation, namely that the bonds Jeremiah made at the beginning of Jehoiakim's reign and sent them in the first year of Zedekiah's reign — but there is no reason for this emendation. And there was a great commentator in Spain, and he interpreted books with grammatical precision, and he interpreted and said: Zedekiah in place of Jehoiakim, and thus he said: \"and after Absalom he did not turn,\" for it is Solomon, and \"on the seventh day\" which is the fourth, \"he set bounds around the mountain\" which is the people, and thus many words like \"two hundred\" — and he carries them all with the wind, for how is it possible in language that a person speaks one word and intends another?! And the one who says this is counted among the madmen, and I have already interpreted all of them, and it would be better for him to say \"I do not know\" than to turn the words of the living Elohim."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:20:08.739075158Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}